  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nTemperatures: Trending Warmer and then Back to Normal\n\nWhile northwesterly flow aloft looks to dominate the weather pattern \nover the next few days, heights aloft will tend to build overall, \nparticularly Sunday into Monday. As this occurs, northern stream \nwave looks to skirt to our north with the associated surface low \nbeing centered around Lake of the Woods around midday Monday. This \nwill lead to a bit of a southwesterly breeze on Monday as well. The \nend result should be temperatures around 60 with readings likely (70-\n90% per 06.13z NBM) above that south of Interstate 94. As broad \ncyclonic flow over the eastern half of Canada expands and multiple \nwaves pass downstream over the region, heights aloft and, \nconsequently, temperatures moderate closer to normal (around 50) as \nnext week goes on.\n\nPrecipitation: Very Likely Dry, Very Low (<5%) Potential for \nAccumulating Snow Wednesday Night\n\nLack of moisture through much of the column will very likely prevent \nprecipitation from occurring even as a series of upper impulses move \nover or near the forecast area Sunday, Monday, and next \nWednesday/Thursday, although a few light showers could occur with \nthe Wednesday/Thursday wave as moisture increases around 850mb and \nCAA aloft leads to shallow surface-based instability. That said, an \ninteresting outlier is the 06.12 GFS, which has a much stronger \nupper wave diving over northeast Iowa and cooler temperatures \nthrough the profile. Should this occur, an axis of accumulating snow \nwould result. With the 06.13z NBM and the 06.00z LREF having almost \nzero chance of snow, safe to say this is a very low (<5%) \nprobability outcome at this time.",
